  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Assistance to Conflict-Affected Population through Cash-Based Interventions in South Sudan</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">With the crisis in Sudan, the influx of people fleeing the country has significantly increased, with more than 35,000 individuals, including 90 per cent of South Sudanese nationals, crossing border entry points along the Sudan-South Sudan border. The conflict has already affected South Sudan&#x2019;s economic outlook, particularly for northern states that heavily rely on imports from Sudan, leading to an increase in food prices and fuel costs and exacerbating protection risks, food insecurity and negative coping mechanisms. Resources are currently limited to provide support to stranded returnees from border points to areas of origin or relocation, calling for a need to enhance cash-based interventions. 
 
The International Organization for Migration (IOM) is supporting the efforts of the Government of South Sudan to address the most urgent basic needs of returnees arriving from Sudan and internally displaced persons affected by floods and conflict. In coordination with the Cash Working Group, IOM is providing basic needs assistance to 40,000 highly vulnerable returnees and internally displaced persons through cash-based interventions in areas with functional markets and in-kind support.</narrative>
  </description>
